# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Local deployment using TDAI shared images — Environment variable template
#
# Usage:
#   cp .env.example .env
#   # Edit .env and replace REPLACE_ME with your own values
#   ./start-all.sh
#
# Two independent configuration groups:
#   MEMORY_* → Used by memory + memory-hub (internal memory/knowledge LLM)
#   PROXY_*  → Used by proxy (upstream LLM receiving forwarded user requests)
# The groups can share values or use entirely different models/providers.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Image tags ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults to public Docker Hub images (amd64 + arm64; no login required to pull):
#   https://hub.docker.com/r/agentmemory/memory-core
#   https://hub.docker.com/r/agentmemory/memory-proxy
#   https://hub.docker.com/r/agentmemory/memory-hub
# To pin a version, replace :latest with a specific tag, such as :1.0.0-beta.1.
# Note: if an image with the same :latest tag already exists locally, the scripts
# reuse it without checking for remote updates.
# To pull the latest version, run `PULL=1 ./start-all.sh`.
MEMORY_CORE_IMAGE=agentmemory/memory-core:latest
MEMORY_HUB_IMAGE=agentmemory/memory-hub:latest
PROXY_IMAGE=agentmemory/memory-proxy:latest

# ── Tencent internal network alternative (for internal staff) ────────────────
# On Tencent's office network/devcloud, use the private mirrors.tencent.com
# registry instead of public Docker Hub. Run `docker login mirrors.tencent.com`
# before using these images.
# MEMORY_CORE_IMAGE=mirrors.tencent.com/memory-team-control/memory-core:latest
# MEMORY_HUB_IMAGE=mirrors.tencent.com/memory-team-control/memory-hub:latest
# PROXY_IMAGE=mirrors.tencent.com/memory-team-control/proxy:latest

# ── Memory group: LLM called internally by memory + memory-hub ───────────────
# Used for memory embedding/summarization and knowledge wiki ingestion/
# summarization in memory-hub.
MEMORY_LLM_BASE_URL=REPLACE_ME              # Example: https://api.deepseek.com/v1
MEMORY_LLM_API_KEY=REPLACE_ME               # Example: sk-xxxxxxxx
MEMORY_LLM_MODEL=REPLACE_ME                 # Example: deepseek-chat
MEMORY_LLM_PROTOCOL=openai                  # openai | anthropic

# ── Proxy group: upstream LLM receiving forwarded user requests ──────────────
# Coding agents/clients call proxy:8096, which forwards their requests here.
PROXY_UPSTREAM_URL=REPLACE_ME               # Example: https://api.deepseek.com/v1
PROXY_UPSTREAM_API_KEY=REPLACE_ME           # Example: sk-xxxxxxxx (may differ from the memory group)
PROXY_UPSTREAM_MODEL=REPLACE_ME             # Example: deepseek-chat

# ── Ports (defaults work as-is; change these if local ports are in use) ──────
MEMORY_CORE_PORT=8420
PANEL_PORT=8125
KNOWLEDGE_PORT=8424
PROXY_PORT=8096

# ── Externally reachable knowledge URL (required by memory-hub) ──────────────
# Lets agents/gateways access the Knowledge Service (KS) tools API.
# Must include the /v3 path prefix.
# For local deployments, host.docker.internal lets containers reach the host.
KNOWLEDGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8424/v3

# ── Base URL shown in the Panel UI's "Client connection address" card ────────
# For local open-source deployments only.
# Core and proxy run separately; coding agents connect to proxy, not core/gateway.
# This value only builds URLs displayed in the Panel UI, such as the baseUrl
# copy cards for CodeBuddy/ClaudeCode. Panel backend requests to the Kernel
# always use REMOTE_INSTANCE_URL (=memory-core), regardless of this variable.
#
# If unset, the script detects the host's LAN IP using `hostname -I` or
# `ipconfig getifaddr en0` on macOS and builds `http://<lan-ip>:${PROXY_PORT}`.
# Users on other computers can connect using the URL copied from the card.
# Explicitly setting an empty value (="") restores the previous gateway_endpoint behavior.
# To specify an address, such as a public domain or a particular network
# interface's IP, uncomment the following line and enter the actual value:
# MEMORY_HUB_PROXY_PUBLIC_URL=http://192.168.1.100:8096

# ── Data volume names ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MEMORY_CORE_VOLUME=tdai-memory-core-data
PANEL_VOLUME=tdai-panel-data

# ── memory-core storage backend (optional; defaults to sqlite) ───────────────
# sqlite  — No extra dependencies; data is stored in MEMORY_CORE_VOLUME.
#           Default for open-source single-machine deployments; recommended to start.
# mongodb — Stores L0/L1/profile/skill documents in MongoDB and uses mongot's
#           native BM25 search. Requires MongoDB 7.0+ with mongot
#           (Atlas or mongodb-atlas-local).
#           On its first connection, core checks the topology and mongot.
#           Initialization fails if mongot is missing; there is no silent fallback.
#
# Recommended approach: this feature is experimental and disabled by default.
# To use MongoDB, run ./start-all-mongo.sh instead of changing this setting here.
# It writes MEMORY_CORE_STORE_MODE=mongodb to this file and reuses the complete
# start-all.sh workflow. You can leave the setting below commented out.
# Uncomment it only if MongoDB has already been enabled or you want to use
# MongoDB directly through ./start-all.sh:
# MEMORY_CORE_STORE_MODE=mongodb
#
# Option A: connect to an external MongoDB deployment (Atlas or a self-hosted
# replica set with mongot). Set the following two variables:
# MONGODB_ENDPOINT=mongodb://user:pass@your-mongo:27017/?directConnection=true
# MONGODB_DATABASE=tdai_memory
#
# Option B: leave MONGODB_ENDPOINT empty. The script starts a local atlas-local
# container on the same network, bundling mongod + mongot. Data persists in
# mongo-local-* volumes; stop-all.sh --purge removes these volumes as well.
# MONGO_LOCAL_IMAGE=mongodb/mongodb-atlas-local:8.3
# MONGO_LOCAL_CONTAINER=tdai-mongo-local
#
# Metadata backend (meta_*: teams/users/agents/tasks/ACLs):
#   auto (default) — Follows MEMORY_CORE_STORE_MODE. In MongoDB mode, uses the
#                    same MongoDB deployment with database tdai_metadata_<instance>,
#                    separate from the tdai_memory data database.
#                    Metadata uses multi-document transactions, so the target must
#                    be a replica set; atlas-local's single-node replica set qualifies.
#   sqlite         — Forces metadata to stay in SQLite on the core container's
#                    volume (previous behavior).
# MEMORY_CORE_METADATA_BACKEND=auto
# To store metadata in another MongoDB cluster, override this setting separately:
# TDAI_METADATA_MONGO_URI=mongodb://user:pass@other-mongo:27017/

# ── memory-core internal credentials ────────────────────────────────────────
# Administrative credential for the Gateway's Bearer authentication layer.
#   Empty (default) — Disables the Bearer gate for a zero-configuration local setup.
#                     Proxy auth/sessionInit can connect directly to memory-core
#                     only with this configuration; see the comments at the top
#                     of start-memory-core.sh.
#   Nonempty        — All requests must include `Authorization: Bearer <key>`.
#                     Proxy currently does not send this header, so its
#                     auth/sessionInit requests will fail.
# For production, fix the proxy integration before enabling a nonempty key.
MEMORY_CORE_GATEWAY_API_KEY=

# Authentication key between the Panel and Knowledge services.
#   Empty (default) — The startup script generates a random value (ks-svc-*) and
#                     writes it back to this file. The same value is passed to
#                     the memory-hub container twice: KNOWLEDGE_SERVICE_KEY
#                     (verified by Knowledge) and KNOWLEDGE_AUTH_TOKEN (used by Panel).
#   Explicit value  — The script keeps the existing value. Distribute it yourself
#                     for multi-machine deployments or external orchestration.
# When configured, Knowledge write/admin endpoints (wiki ingestion,
# llm-binding/set, etc.) require `Authorization: Bearer <key>`.
# tools/* and read-only query endpoints remain open.
KNOWLEDGE_SERVICE_KEY=

# Admin username (used by the initial init-admin call).
# On first startup, the script generates a random user_key with the sk-mem-
# prefix and saves it in ./.admin-key. Subsequent scripts/claude-proxy read
# the key from this file; manual entry is no longer required.
MEMORY_CORE_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin

# ── Memory prompt mode ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Selects the prompt family for the L1/L2/L3 pipeline:
#   code (default) — For software engineering/team collaboration; extracts
#                    project facts, tasks, decisions, standard operating
#                    procedures (SOPs), constraints, etc.
#   chat           — For personal conversations/teaching/casual chat; extracts
#                    persona, episodic, and instruction memories.
# Note: casual chat in code mode may produce no memories if the LLM finds
# no engineering content worth retaining.
MEMORY_PROMPT_MODE=code

# Set to 0 for RAG-only deployment; skips proxy startup and LLM prompts.
PROXY_ENABLED=1
